e of Sequences Containing Power and Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9acbaec-6d14-5257-adb4-f158aed059e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269259} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2b696b-df5a-53b1-b376-dd2a209a7d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269260} {:answer/id #uuid "a9267d48-16c7-5c66-993c-a073c5344930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269261} {:answer/id #uuid "f3355a7d-ae0e-54dd-b85e-a28eb3e35923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269262} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbdf5fa-ad68-558f-a7ec-1d5a7c957c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcbdf5fa-ad68-558f-a7ec-1d5a7c957c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269263} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9c98ebd-735b-5550-9239-00b7670a40d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269258}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b24ca1a-e901-5db7-8874-19efca043c3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75723" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1$ for $n>1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43f839b4-2b0e-5ab5-809a-741747f1016d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Power and Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c4491e-1c53-58b0-a80e-bb464838ec23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269266} {:answer/id #uuid "fb35f4bd-fd87-5829-96ca-a1c2b4028857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269267} {:answer/id #uuid "fa66cd2b-72b0-5bf7-9221-f055d55ca7f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} {:answer/id #uuid "a82d3740-d2df-5ed2-9159-6b908f90ab81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269269} {:answer/id #uuid "92e6aa0d-d562-5579-92dd-2c6833928fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92e6aa0d-d562-5579-92dd-2c6833928fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269270} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3a3cd68-6b68-5d3d-b4dd-d3435911477e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269265}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97db878f-1117-561e-b854-77336879e030" :question/math-academy-id "q-75727" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e00ff7-a91e-5b56-9f3a-24e7318cf004" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence Sequences Containing Power and Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87cc7b3e-b208-53ce-82f0-eca97793810c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269273} {:answer/id #uuid "487d9cce-0d9b-574e-af54-d257fb58e666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269274} {:answer/id #uuid "0f647064-e09b-55aa-b9dc-aec6cc65da61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269275} {:answer/id #uuid "63b46622-c486-5e3c-a8f1-d7bdb62a514a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269276} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c666a4-b4a4-5743-97eb-e225fcf4ba86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63b46622-c486-5e3c-a8f1-d7bdb62a514a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269276} :answer-field/id #uuid "9843ab96-d78f-5b14-830d-2ad29cee52c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-fi